Anchor: Step 1  
Location: “We verify that no integer \(d\) with \(2 \le d \le 6\) divides \(7\), using the Division Algorithm: \[
7 = 2 \cdot 3 + 1,\quad r_2 = 1 \ne 0,\quad\text{so }2\nmid 7;
\] …”  
Description: Justification Gap. To use the Division Algorithm remainder criterion, the proof must establish that each displayed \(r_d\) is the unique remainder satisfying \(0 \le r_d < d\). The proof lists the decompositions and notes \(r_d\ne 0\), but does not explicitly verify the required remainder bounds for each \(d=2,3,4,5,6\).

Anchor: Step 1  
Location: “Since \(7 > 0\) and \(d > 0\), we have \(k > 0\), hence \(k \ge 1\), giving \(d = 7/k \le 7/1 = 7\).”  
Description: Justification Gap. The inference from \(7=dk\), \(7>0\), and \(d>0\) to \(k>0\) is not explicitly justified by a stated sign rule or quotient argument. The subsequent implications \(k>0 \Rightarrow k\ge 1\) for an integer \(k\), and \(k\ge 1 \Rightarrow 7/k\le 7/1\), also rely on unstated order/discreteness and reciprocal-monotonicity facts.

Anchor: Step 1  
Location: “Thus every positive divisor \(d > 1\) of \(7\) satisfies \(2 \le d \le 7\). … Every positive divisor \(d > 1\) of \(7\) satisfies \(2 \le d \le 7\), and we have checked all cases \(d \in \{2,3,4,5,6,7\}\).”  
Description: Justification Gap. The proof does not explicitly justify that an integer satisfying \(2 \le d \le 7\) must be one of \(2,3,4,5,6,7\). This uses the discreteness/order structure of the integers, which was not stated as a permitted fact.

Anchor: Step 1  
Location: “Replacing \((a, b)\) by \((-a, -b)\) if necessary—note that \((-a)/(-b) = a/b\), so the equality is preserved—we may assume \(b > 0\).”  
Description: Justification Gap. The equality preservation is justified, but the proof does not explicitly justify that this replacement makes the denominator positive when \(b<0\). It relies on the unstated order fact that if \(b<0\), then \(-b>0\).

Anchor: Step 5  
Location: “Finally, \(q_0 = 7m\) and \(7 \ge 2 > 1\), so \(1/7 < 1\), giving \(m = q_0/7 < q_0 \cdot 1 = q_0\).”  
Description: Justification Gap. The inequalities \(1/7<1\) and \(q_0/7<q_0\) rely on unstated reciprocal/order and multiplication-by-positive order properties. The permitted facts do not explicitly establish these steps, so the conclusion \(m<q_0\) is not fully justified.
